WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.324 --> 00:00:03.771
(intense music)

00:00:03.771 --> 00:00:06.587
- [Man] I remember hearing
stories about Witchers.

00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:09.050
Is it true what they say?

00:00:11.650 --> 00:00:14.900
- I'm gonna bring showrunner
out, Lauren Hissrich,

00:00:14.900 --> 00:00:16.800
and star of the show Freya Allen,

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:19.170
please give them a very
warm round of applause,

00:00:19.170 --> 00:00:20.913
go for it, here they come!

00:00:20.913 --> 00:00:23.690
(crowd cheers)

00:00:23.690 --> 00:00:25.240
- I had read the book The Last Wish

00:00:25.240 --> 00:00:26.760
before Netflix had ever approached me,

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:27.740
and I'd really loved it.

00:00:27.740 --> 00:00:30.110
It's a book of just short
stories that follow Geralt

00:00:30.110 --> 00:00:32.293
as a monster hunter, as a Witcher.

00:00:33.430 --> 00:00:35.810
And there was something really exciting

00:00:35.810 --> 00:00:39.966
about taking this world
that is high fantasy,

00:00:39.966 --> 00:00:41.770
it obviously has a lot of monsters,

00:00:41.770 --> 00:00:43.860
and has a lot of magic,
and has a lot of fun.

00:00:43.860 --> 00:00:46.970
But at the core off it are these
really grounded characters.

00:00:46.970 --> 00:00:48.610
And the thing that excited me the most,

00:00:48.610 --> 00:00:50.030
when I went to pitch Netflix,

00:00:50.030 --> 00:00:52.630
is telling the story
of Geralt the Witcher,

00:00:52.630 --> 00:00:55.300
but also telling the stories
of Ciri and Yennefer,

00:00:55.300 --> 00:00:57.120
who are big players in the books,

00:00:57.120 --> 00:01:00.190
but aren't quite given the
same introduction as Geralt is.

00:01:00.190 --> 00:01:01.670
It's interesting, because people ask me

00:01:01.670 --> 00:01:02.960
about the female characters a lot,

00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:05.670
and sort of the complications of them.

00:01:05.670 --> 00:01:08.110
But it's really just what real women

00:01:08.110 --> 00:01:09.740
are like anyway right?

00:01:09.740 --> 00:01:12.210
I mean it's strong in some circumstances,

00:01:12.210 --> 00:01:13.240
vulnerable in others.

00:01:13.240 --> 00:01:16.800
Occasionally bold, occasionally
bitchy, occasionally silent.

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:19.860
To me, what I wanted to
capture is these women,

00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:23.110
before they were seen through
the lens of anyone else.

00:01:23.110 --> 00:01:26.260
In the books, all of the
characters are met through Geralt.

00:01:26.260 --> 00:01:27.980
So you get Geralt's impression of them,

00:01:27.980 --> 00:01:29.680
and then you get to learn to meet them.

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:33.100
And I wanted the world to
see Ciri, who she was first.

00:01:33.100 --> 00:01:35.010
I wanted to introduce the world

00:01:35.010 --> 00:01:36.910
to the three of them at the same time,

00:01:36.910 --> 00:01:38.707
and allow them each to really flourish

00:01:38.707 --> 00:01:40.690
as characters on their own,

00:01:40.690 --> 00:01:42.240
before they ever started meeting

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:43.790
or interacting with each other.

00:01:44.630 --> 00:01:46.120
When I first was pitching to Netflix,

00:01:46.120 --> 00:01:48.830
I pitched them a completely
different version of the story,

00:01:48.830 --> 00:01:51.100
which I've actually never
talked about before.

00:01:51.100 --> 00:01:52.270
I pitched them a version of the story

00:01:52.270 --> 00:01:54.490
that actually started with
the second to the last book,

00:01:54.490 --> 00:01:55.853
which is Lady of the Lake.

00:01:57.300 --> 00:02:01.830
Where Ciri sort of ends
up sitting by a lake,

00:02:01.830 --> 00:02:02.663
meeting with a man,

00:02:02.663 --> 00:02:03.950
and sort of telling her story.

00:02:03.950 --> 00:02:06.070
And I actually started with
that narrative structure,

00:02:06.070 --> 00:02:08.720
which is to have Ciri
be our focal character

00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:10.520
and be kind of our narrator.

00:02:10.520 --> 00:02:11.640
The problem was is that,

00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:13.850
I realized as I started
writing that version,

00:02:13.850 --> 00:02:15.380
that I wasn't getting enough of Geralt

00:02:15.380 --> 00:02:16.213
and his perspective

00:02:16.213 --> 00:02:17.950
because it was all
being told through Ciri.

00:02:17.950 --> 00:02:19.550
So it actually took me a while,

00:02:19.550 --> 00:02:20.770
I went through several versions.

00:02:20.770 --> 00:02:22.390
And then I literally had

00:02:22.390 --> 00:02:24.450
one of those aha moments in the shower,

00:02:24.450 --> 00:02:26.650
and sort of jumped out
of the shower (laughing)

00:02:26.650 --> 00:02:29.150
and said to my husband,
like does this make sense?

00:02:30.010 --> 00:02:32.620
Can I tell all three of these
stories at the same time

00:02:32.620 --> 00:02:35.270
and now have it be too confusing?

00:02:35.270 --> 00:02:37.480
This particular season
skips through a lot of space

00:02:37.480 --> 00:02:40.040
and time as well, so I think there'll be

00:02:40.040 --> 00:02:43.100
a lot of fun pairings of relationships,

00:02:43.100 --> 00:02:46.080
some that fans will hopefully
really be looking forward to

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:49.380
and expecting, and also some new fun ones.

00:02:49.380 --> 00:02:50.910
You know, it's been interesting.

00:02:50.910 --> 00:02:53.330
Because I do think that
television audiences

00:02:53.330 --> 00:02:54.770
are really savvy now.

00:02:54.770 --> 00:02:58.270
And as a writer, as a creator,
we have that on our side.

00:02:58.270 --> 00:03:01.510
So I think that we do
jump through time a lot,

00:03:01.510 --> 00:03:02.970
and we jump back and forth through events

00:03:02.970 --> 00:03:05.470
and characters that seem dead aren't dead,

00:03:05.470 --> 00:03:07.920
and people come back
and you revisit places

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:09.640
or revisit stories again.

00:03:09.640 --> 00:03:12.490
But I think it's an
exciting journey for fans

00:03:12.490 --> 00:03:14.230
and for even audiences

00:03:14.230 --> 00:03:16.010
that have never seen The Witcher before.

00:03:16.010 --> 00:03:19.640
Because, I think that they can keep up,

00:03:19.640 --> 00:03:21.340
I think they're really really smart,

00:03:21.340 --> 00:03:23.510
and want to be challenged
in how they watch television

00:03:23.510 --> 00:03:24.975
and I think this can do that.

00:03:24.975 --> 00:03:26.634
(dramatic music)

00:03:26.634 --> 00:03:27.570
(sword clangs)

00:03:27.570 --> 00:03:30.582
- I've heard tales of your kind Witcher.

00:03:30.582 --> 00:03:34.530
(sword slashing)
(men exclaiming)

00:03:34.530 --> 00:03:37.423
I thought you'd have fangs
or horns or something.

00:03:38.750 --> 00:03:40.847
- I had them filed down.

00:03:40.847 --> 00:03:42.910
(Yennefer laughs)

00:03:42.910 --> 00:03:44.150
- You know, basically when we meet Geralt,

00:03:44.150 --> 00:03:46.070
he's almost 100 years old already,

00:03:46.070 --> 00:03:48.150
and he's lived a lot of life.

00:03:48.150 --> 00:03:51.270
And I wanted to start him
in a place where I could,

00:03:51.270 --> 00:03:52.103
that an audience could sort of

00:03:52.103 --> 00:03:53.580
understand what he's gone through.

00:03:53.580 --> 00:03:56.090
But then immediately challenge
him in that first episode,

00:03:56.090 --> 00:03:59.133
and sort of start his journey
off in a different direction.

00:04:00.070 --> 00:04:03.060
So I chose a story called The Last Wish.

00:04:03.060 --> 00:04:04.550
Which to me, sort of puts him up

00:04:04.550 --> 00:04:06.290
against a character named Renfri.

00:04:06.290 --> 00:04:09.140
And challenges his worldview.

00:04:09.140 --> 00:04:10.910
So basically we get to meet the Witcher,

00:04:10.910 --> 00:04:13.860
we get to hear how he exists in the world,

00:04:13.860 --> 00:04:16.680
what he thinks his role
is, how he feels about it,

00:04:16.680 --> 00:04:18.590
and then we immediately challenge it

00:04:18.590 --> 00:04:20.110
and make him rethink that.

00:04:20.110 --> 00:04:22.880
And that really starts his journey off,

00:04:22.880 --> 00:04:25.640
I would say his
interactions with Renfri are

00:04:25.640 --> 00:04:28.840
sort of his emotional touchstone
for the entire season,

00:04:28.840 --> 00:04:31.160
and he comes back pretty
often to what happened

00:04:31.160 --> 00:04:32.550
and the decision that he made.

00:04:32.550 --> 00:04:34.300
This is Geralt's sort of first run in

00:04:34.300 --> 00:04:35.870
with destiny in the show,

00:04:35.870 --> 00:04:37.470
and it really changes his journey

00:04:37.470 --> 00:04:39.000
for the entire rest of the season.

00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:40.400
Also it's just one of my favorite stories,

00:04:40.400 --> 00:04:42.380
Renfri is one of my
favorite characters as well.

00:04:42.380 --> 00:04:43.620
So to introduce audiences

00:04:43.620 --> 00:04:48.260
to sort of this kick-ass
female warrior bandit,

00:04:48.260 --> 00:04:49.420
and say these are the type of characters

00:04:49.420 --> 00:04:50.630
that you're gonna see in our series,

00:04:50.630 --> 00:04:53.790
and there're gonna be people that seem bad

00:04:53.790 --> 00:04:54.623
that are actually good,

00:04:54.623 --> 00:04:56.520
and people who are good that,

00:04:56.520 --> 00:04:57.710
or seem good who actually

00:04:57.710 --> 00:04:59.980
have much more complicated motivations,

00:04:59.980 --> 00:05:01.720
and I think it's a really good way

00:05:01.720 --> 00:05:03.810
to present the world of
The Witcher to an audience,

00:05:03.810 --> 00:05:07.800
cause it's morally and
ethically gray from the get-go.

00:05:07.800 --> 00:05:09.640
Just because some one
looks good on the outside,

00:05:09.640 --> 00:05:10.900
or may have good intentions,

00:05:10.900 --> 00:05:13.760
doesn't mean they don't
have a darker side inside.

00:05:13.760 --> 00:05:15.240
And the people that you would

00:05:15.240 --> 00:05:16.790
typically watch the first season

00:05:16.790 --> 00:05:18.780
and say, well oh obviously
that's the villain,

00:05:18.780 --> 00:05:21.150
it was very important for
us to get into his brain

00:05:21.150 --> 00:05:23.240
and understand why he's
doing what he's doing,

00:05:23.240 --> 00:05:25.470
and that he actually thinks
that he's doing something

00:05:25.470 --> 00:05:26.900
for the good of the continent.

00:05:26.900 --> 00:05:29.500
You know, one of the first
things that I wanted to do is,

00:05:29.500 --> 00:05:31.590
there's a lot of history revealed,

00:05:31.590 --> 00:05:34.300
and sort of backstories of characters,

00:05:34.300 --> 00:05:37.740
that are revealed through
little lines of dialogue.

00:05:37.740 --> 00:05:38.960
And it will be Yennefer saying,

00:05:38.960 --> 00:05:40.950
you know, as a child this happened.

00:05:40.950 --> 00:05:44.240
And I thought, well rather
than be told that story,

00:05:44.240 --> 00:05:45.490
I wanna see that story.

00:05:45.490 --> 00:05:47.900
Which is part of the reason we
went back in Yen's childhood,

00:05:47.900 --> 00:05:50.610
or part of the reason that we
start with Ciri as a princess.

00:05:50.610 --> 00:05:53.320
And talking to her family
before she goes out

00:05:53.320 --> 00:05:55.880
and sort of starts her
journey in the books.

00:05:55.880 --> 00:05:59.550
I prefer our audience to
evolve with our characters,

00:05:59.550 --> 00:06:01.970
and so that everyone's
kind of on the same page

00:06:01.970 --> 00:06:03.470
in moving forward through stories,

00:06:03.470 --> 00:06:05.030
not necessarily in a linear fashion,

00:06:05.030 --> 00:06:08.560
but in a way where an
audience member understands

00:06:08.560 --> 00:06:11.980
how this character got from
point A to point B emotionally.

00:06:11.980 --> 00:06:16.050
The big thing that I learned
on Daredevil and Defenders

00:06:16.050 --> 00:06:17.810
and Umbrella Academy is about adaptation,

00:06:17.810 --> 00:06:20.040
and about taking something
that has a huge fan base

00:06:20.040 --> 00:06:23.910
and that people really really
love in its existing form,

00:06:23.910 --> 00:06:25.623
and translating it to a new form.

00:06:26.550 --> 00:06:28.830
And the changes that have to be made

00:06:28.830 --> 00:06:30.360
between a comic book and television,

00:06:30.360 --> 00:06:33.140
or between a beloved book
series and television.

00:06:33.140 --> 00:06:35.080
You know, I definitely understand

00:06:35.080 --> 00:06:37.750
that people who have
been reading these books

00:06:37.750 --> 00:06:38.583
since they were kids,

00:06:38.583 --> 00:06:39.910
or have read them to their kids,

00:06:39.910 --> 00:06:41.200
it's really scary to say,

00:06:41.200 --> 00:06:42.440
oh we're gonna have someone new come in

00:06:42.440 --> 00:06:45.940
and just start changing things
in this thing that I love.

00:06:45.940 --> 00:06:47.190
What I will always say is,

00:06:47.190 --> 00:06:50.570
in general we add to, we
try not to detract from.

00:06:50.570 --> 00:06:51.930
Sometimes we have to leave things out,

00:06:51.930 --> 00:06:56.620
again 4000 pages of books, only
eight hours to tell a story.

00:06:56.620 --> 00:06:59.580
So there's a lot that
we have to leave out.

00:06:59.580 --> 00:07:02.180
When we start inventing new
characters specifically,

00:07:02.180 --> 00:07:05.350
it's usually just to give our
characters someone to talk to,

00:07:05.350 --> 00:07:07.630
in a book, you don't necessarily
have to talk to people,

00:07:07.630 --> 00:07:10.560
you can have pages and
pages of description.

00:07:10.560 --> 00:07:11.950
When you're on the screen,

00:07:11.950 --> 00:07:14.050
we can't just get into a character's mind

00:07:14.050 --> 00:07:15.520
and understand what they're feeling.

00:07:15.520 --> 00:07:16.970
So we have to give those characters

00:07:16.970 --> 00:07:18.920
someone to bounce ideas off of.

00:07:18.920 --> 00:07:21.633
There are entire chapters
of the book that are,

00:07:23.000 --> 00:07:25.510
Geralt just kind of espousing things,

00:07:25.510 --> 00:07:27.730
well you can't just put a
character down in a room

00:07:27.730 --> 00:07:30.150
and have him start telling, you know,

00:07:30.150 --> 00:07:32.810
monologuing for six, seven, eight pages,

00:07:32.810 --> 00:07:34.350
it just doesn't work on screen.

00:07:34.350 --> 00:07:38.193
- [Woman] Chaos is the most
dangerous thing in this world.

00:07:39.260 --> 00:07:42.846
But, without control, chaos will kill you.

00:07:42.846 --> 00:07:45.596
(dramatic music)

00:07:47.090 --> 00:07:49.790
- Obviously there's a lot
of allusions to fairy tales.

00:07:50.740 --> 00:07:52.350
The Last Wish, very much has a

00:07:52.350 --> 00:07:54.050
Snow White and the Seven
Dwarves things happening,

00:07:54.050 --> 00:07:55.570
which is great.

00:07:55.570 --> 00:07:57.070
There's a story called A Grain of Truth,

00:07:57.070 --> 00:07:59.630
which is a little bit of
a Beauty and the Beast.

00:07:59.630 --> 00:08:01.970
And a lot of the monsters and characters

00:08:01.970 --> 00:08:04.260
are drawn directly from Slavic folklore,

00:08:04.260 --> 00:08:06.150
and that was really
important for us to keep

00:08:06.150 --> 00:08:07.120
and sort of honor.

00:08:07.120 --> 00:08:09.100
And even as we create new monsters,

00:08:09.100 --> 00:08:10.520
or new characters that aren't in the books

00:08:10.520 --> 00:08:12.040
to sort of flesh out stories,

00:08:12.040 --> 00:08:15.820
we actually go back to
sort of Polish culture

00:08:15.820 --> 00:08:17.210
as often as we can.

00:08:17.210 --> 00:08:19.880
We have a Polish producer
Tomasz Baginski who's amazing,

00:08:19.880 --> 00:08:22.810
and who is my immediate go-to

00:08:22.810 --> 00:08:25.200
for so many questions
about so many things.

00:08:25.200 --> 00:08:26.730
And sometimes it's just about,

00:08:26.730 --> 00:08:28.430
what are the stories that
you were told as a kid,

00:08:28.430 --> 00:08:29.740
what are the ghost stories you were told?

00:08:29.740 --> 00:08:31.490
What has been passed
down through your family?

00:08:31.490 --> 00:08:34.650
The other thing that is
unexpected in The Witcher,

00:08:34.650 --> 00:08:37.320
is the wit and humor.

00:08:37.320 --> 00:08:41.610
I think fantasy a lot of times
is really really earnest.

00:08:41.610 --> 00:08:44.530
It's about big, sort
of life changing stuff.

00:08:44.530 --> 00:08:46.290
Battles and wars being fought,

00:08:46.290 --> 00:08:48.720
and people being marginalized.

00:08:48.720 --> 00:08:51.280
And all of those things
exist in The Witcher,

00:08:51.280 --> 00:08:54.840
but at the same time, the
thing that I connected

00:08:54.840 --> 00:08:56.540
to most in the books is that,

00:08:56.540 --> 00:08:59.280
you also really get to know characters

00:08:59.280 --> 00:09:00.850
who aren't being directly impacted

00:09:00.850 --> 00:09:02.750
by that battle happening over there.

00:09:02.750 --> 00:09:04.100
They just have to get up every day

00:09:04.100 --> 00:09:07.940
and keep their farming
going, and continue to live.

00:09:07.940 --> 00:09:11.170
And what I love about
how real people exist is,

00:09:11.170 --> 00:09:13.550
in the face of tragedy,
we often turn to humor,

00:09:13.550 --> 00:09:15.370
it's our only way to keep going.

00:09:15.370 --> 00:09:17.750
And the characters do that
all the time in The Witcher,

00:09:17.750 --> 00:09:20.470
and it's a really nice balance I think,

00:09:20.470 --> 00:09:23.250
because you get to laugh
as much as anything else.

00:09:23.250 --> 00:09:25.753
It's not really depressing to
watch, at the end of the day.

00:09:26.717 --> 00:09:30.550
(sword clanging and slashing)

00:09:31.698 --> 00:09:32.553
- Don't judge me.

00:09:34.660 --> 00:09:37.400
- So I first met Andrzej in Poland,

00:09:37.400 --> 00:09:38.780
right after I had sold the series.

00:09:38.780 --> 00:09:40.320
And we sat down and we had a lovely lunch,

00:09:40.320 --> 00:09:43.700
and I sort of picked his
brain about who he was

00:09:43.700 --> 00:09:44.920
and where he'd come from,

00:09:44.920 --> 00:09:48.280
and not even so much about what
made him write The Witcher,

00:09:48.280 --> 00:09:51.540
I'm sure he's been asked that
question a million times.

00:09:51.540 --> 00:09:52.890
But it was really just sort of

00:09:52.890 --> 00:09:54.560
trying to understand who he was,

00:09:54.560 --> 00:09:57.633
and who he is in these books,
where is he in these books?

00:09:59.060 --> 00:10:01.930
And then he came to Budapest
where we were shooting,

00:10:01.930 --> 00:10:03.600
it was amazing.

00:10:03.600 --> 00:10:05.330
He was really quite taken,

00:10:05.330 --> 00:10:09.350
and he got tears in
his eyes several times,

00:10:09.350 --> 00:10:12.090
and would bat them away. (laughs)

00:10:12.090 --> 00:10:15.100
I think it was really
great for him to sort of,

00:10:15.100 --> 00:10:18.520
for him to see that we were
honoring his vision so much.

00:10:18.520 --> 00:10:20.700
And sort of bringing to life,

00:10:20.700 --> 00:10:22.470
and I think everyone's always worried too

00:10:22.470 --> 00:10:25.780
that we're not going to be
able to do things justice,

00:10:25.780 --> 00:10:26.970
and I think he felt very much

00:10:26.970 --> 00:10:30.300
like we were doing his stories justice,

00:10:30.300 --> 00:10:35.010
and making them even more
beautiful than he'd anticipated.

00:10:35.010 --> 00:10:37.563
The best thing that he said to me,

00:10:39.260 --> 00:10:43.500
I was asking him if he had,
if he'd read all the scripts,

00:10:43.500 --> 00:10:45.940
if he was interested in
starting to see early cuts

00:10:45.940 --> 00:10:47.840
because he basically has access

00:10:47.840 --> 00:10:49.940
to as much of the material as he wants to.

00:10:51.160 --> 00:10:56.160
And he said no, because he
said that he thinks of it

00:10:56.300 --> 00:10:58.260
kind of like the ingredients of soup.

00:10:58.260 --> 00:11:01.140
And he said, if you just line
up the ingredients for me

00:11:01.140 --> 00:11:02.550
that's not gonna do anything for me.

00:11:02.550 --> 00:11:04.980
What I would rather do is
taste the soup in the end.

00:11:04.980 --> 00:11:08.940
And he really wants to, I think
he trusts what we're doing,

00:11:08.940 --> 00:11:11.260
he has seen enough to whet his appetite,

00:11:11.260 --> 00:11:14.010
and now he wants to see what
the world is going to see

00:11:14.010 --> 00:11:15.230
in its fully finished form,

00:11:15.230 --> 00:11:17.913
and I'm so excited for him to see it!

00:11:19.111 --> 00:11:20.327
- [Man] You will unleash

00:11:20.327 --> 00:11:22.124
(intense dramatic music)

00:11:22.124 --> 00:11:24.518
true calamity upon us all.

00:11:24.518 --> 00:11:27.935
(intense dramatic music)

00:11:28.810 --> 00:11:30.505
- [Geralt] I'll take that chance.

00:11:30.505 --> 00:11:33.255
(dramatic music)

